course: everyday
lesson: 282
title: Chain messages, lures and scam messages
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A scam is a trick to take money, information or something else from you. Scammers copy real logos, pretend to be banks, parcel companies, the government, famous YouTubers or even your friends, and they almost always try to make you hurry or feel scared.
explain: Spot the signs: a link to «fix your account», a pop-up with a phone number, a request to pay with gift cards, a threat, or a push to move to a private app. Real businesses never ask to be paid with gift cards, real computers do not warn you through web pop-ups with phone numbers, and real officials do not demand money by surprise calls.
explain: Example: a girl in Bristol gets a message from her friend's account: «Can you buy me a gift card? I'll pay you back». She phones her friend, who says, «That wasn't me — my account was hacked!».
explain: The same tricks work in real life: a stranger offering sweets or a puppy to come with them is a known way to lure children. Never go anywhere with someone you do not know; run to a safe adult.
explain: If someone online threatens you, it is never your fault. Don't reply or send anything, and tell a trusted adult straight away; adults and the police can help.
fact: Always ask a parent before a «free trial» that wants a card number; free trials often turn into paid subscriptions.
fact: No company gives prizes for chain messages, so do not forward them.
fact: Chain messages that say something bad will happen if you don't forward them are tricks; nothing bad will happen.
fact: Never go anywhere with someone you do not know, even if they offer sweets or a puppy; run to a safe adult.
fact: Fake messages copy real logos to trick people into clicking (called phishing), so do not click their links; show an adult.
fact: Texts about a parcel that could not be delivered are a very common scam; adults check deliveries on the official site.
fact: If a pop-up says «Virus detected! Call this number», close the page, tell an adult and never call the number.
fact: Real businesses never ask to be paid with gift cards; scammers like them because they cannot be traced or refunded.
fact: If a friend's account asks for gift cards or money, talk to the friend another way, like in person or by phone.
fact: If an online contact asks you to move to a private app, to meet up or to keep them a secret from your parents, say no and tell a parent; secrets and private apps are danger signs.
fact: If someone online threatens to share something unless you send more, do not reply or send anything and tell a trusted adult at once; it is not your fault.
fact: In the UK you can report to CEOP, in the USA to the NCMEC CyberTipline, in Australia to the eSafety Commissioner and in New Zealand to Netsafe.
fact: Real officials do not demand money by surprise phone calls; give the phone to a parent.
fact: Scammers pretend to be famous YouTubers and footballers giving things away; check their real, official account and never send a fee.
fact: A QR code on an unknown poster can lead to a scam site, so ask an adult before scanning one.
fact: Families can agree a check question or code word to beat «Hi Mum, I've lost my phone» scams.
exercise: A game offers a «free 7-day trial» and asks for your mum's card number. What do you do? | Ask a parent first | Type in the card number quickly | Use a friend's card instead | Sign up and cancel later yourself
exercise: Why can free trials cost money? | They often turn into paid subscriptions if not cancelled | They are always free forever, so nothing is ever charged at all | The company sends you money back | Only adults pay for subscriptions
exercise: A message says «Forward this to 10 friends and win a prize!». What should you do? | Do not forward it; no company gives prizes for chain messages | Forward it to 10 friends at once so you are first to win the prize | Forward it to 20 to win twice | Reply asking which prize you get
exercise: A chain message says «If you don't forward this, you'll have bad luck for 7 years». What is true? | It is a trick to frighten you; nothing bad will happen | You must forward it to be safe, just in case it turns out true | Bad luck only comes if you delete it | It is true if many people sent it
exercise: A smiling stranger in a park in Phoenix says «Come see my puppies in my car». What should you do? | Say no, never go with him, and run to a safe adult | Go quickly, because puppies are cute and he's smiling and friendly | Go if he seems kind and friendly | Ask him to bring the puppies closer
exercise: An email «from Apple» says your account is locked and you must click to fix it. What is right? | Don't click the link; show an adult | Click and enter your password to unlock it | Reply with your address | Forward it to your friends
exercise: What is phishing? | Fake messages copying real logos to trick people | Catching fish with a computer and a long electric line | A safe way to reset passwords | A game for learning to type
exercise: A text says «Your parcel could not be delivered, click here». What do adults do? | Ignore the link and check on the official delivery site | Click it to see what parcel it is and who sent it to them | Pay the small fee it asks for | Reply with their address
exercise: A pop-up flashes «VIRUS DETECTED! Call this number now!». What should you do? | Close the page, tell an adult and never call | Call the number quickly | Type in your parent's card number to pay for the fix | Leave it open and keep playing
exercise: Your friend's account messages you asking you to buy gift cards. What is wise? | Talk to your friend another way, like in person or by phone | Buy the cards to be a good friend, since they asked you nicely | Send the card numbers by message | Ask for more details by message
exercise: Someone wants to be paid with game cards or gift cards. What does that tell you? | It is very likely a scam, as real businesses never ask that | It is the safest way to pay, because nobody can steal a gift card | Gift cards are what shops prefer | It is fine if they say please
exercise: A person you met in a game says «Let's chat on a private app and meet up». What do you do? | Say no and tell a parent | Agree, but only for a short chat | Meet them in a park with a friend | Give them your address to send a gift
exercise: Someone online says they will share a photo unless you send more. What is right? | Don't reply or send anything and tell a trusted adult at once | Send what they ask so it stops | Keep it secret, because you'll be the one who gets in trouble | Try to argue with them yourself
exercise: A message from «MrBeast» says he is giving away money if you send a fee first. What should you do? | Check the real account and send nothing | Send the fee quickly before others do | Send your parent's bank details | Share it with your class
exercise: A caller says he is from the government and your family owes money. What do you do? | Give no information and pass the phone to a parent | Tell him your address so he can send the bill to your parents | Promise him your parents will pay | Ask him to call back when they are out
exercise: Your mum gets a text: «Hi Mum, I've lost my phone, this is my new number, please send money». What can families do? | Agree a check question or code word | Always send money to new numbers | Reply with bank details quickly | Change their own numbers every week
exercise: A poster at the bus stop has a QR code: «Scan to win a prize!». What is wise? | Ask an adult before scanning unknown codes | Scan it at once to win | Scan it twice to win double | Share the code with your friends so you all win a prize together
test: A girl gets a message: «Share this or your phone will explode tonight». What is true? | It is a frightening chain message; nothing will happen | Phones really can explode if you don't share the message in time | She must share it with everyone | She should turn off her phone forever
test: Which request is almost certainly a scam? | Someone asks you to pay them with gift card numbers | A shop asks you to pay at the till | A teacher asks you for a signed permission slip for the school trip | A library asks you to return a book
test: A boy in Sydney gets a call from someone saying he is from the tax office and his parents will be arrested unless they pay today. What should he do? | Give nothing, hang up or pass the phone to a parent | Tell them the family's bank details so his parents are not arrested | Promise to pay with his pocket money | Give them his school's address
test: An online friend says «Don't tell your parents about me, let's talk on another app». Why is this a warning sign? | Moving away from watched places and secrets are danger signs | It is normal for good friends to keep secrets from their parents | Other apps are always faster | Parents don't understand games
test: Someone online has sent a scary threat. Whose fault is it? | Never yours; tell a trusted adult and do not send anything | Yours, so you must fix it alone without telling any grown-up at all | Yours, if you replied to them once | Nobody's, so just ignore it forever
test: A girl's grandma gets an email with the bank's logo asking her to «confirm her PIN». What should she do? | Not click, and contact the bank using its real number | Reply with her PIN to keep her account safe | Click the link quickly before her account is locked for good | Forward it to the family to check
test: A message says a famous footballer is giving away free trainers if you click and pay delivery. How can you check? | Look at his real, official account and send nothing | Pay delivery, since it is cheap | Click to see if the trainers are real | Message the account and give your address so they can post them
test: A van driver offers a boy sweets if he gets in to «help find a lost cat». What is right? | Never go with him, get away and tell a safe adult | Get in the van, because helping to find a lost cat is kind | Get in but keep the door open | Ask for the sweets first, then decide
test: A family agreed that the code word is «pineapple». Mum gets a text from a «new number» asking for money but it doesn't know the word. What does that show? | It is probably a scammer pretending to be her child | Her child forgot the word | The code word was too hard | The phone company changed the number and the code word with it
test: chain msg says forward to 10 ppl or bad luck forever | Ignore it, nothing bad will happen | Forward it fast just to be safe, you never know | Forward it to only five people | Reply asking who wrote it
test: text says my parcel couldnt be delivered, click link. ok? | No, it's a common scam, an adult checks the real site | Yes, click it so your parcel can come tomorrow | Yes, but don't type your name | Reply STOP and then click it
test: pop up says VIRUS call this number now!! | Close it, tell an adult, never call the number | Call fast before the virus spreads to all your stuff | Type your password to scan it | Turn off the computer for a week
test: guy at the park says come see his puppies in the van | No, run to a safe adult and tell them | Go but only for a minute | Go if he seems really kind and has a nice smile | Ask him to bring the puppies out
explain: A girl in Australia got a text about a parcel and clicked the link; it asked for her mum's card details, and the next day money had been taken.
explain: A boy in Ireland forwarded a chain message that said he would have bad luck if he did not; nothing happened to him, but his friends got scared and annoyed.
fact: Scam texts about parcels, prizes and unpaid bills trick many people every year into giving away card details.
fact: Bad luck does not come from not forwarding a message; chain messages only spread worry.
test: text says parcel couldnt be delivered click here | Don't click; show a parent, it's a common scam | Click it so you can find out where your parcel is | Reply with your address | Forward it to friends to check
test: popup says virus detected call this number now | Close it and tell an adult | Call the number quickly | Type in your password to fix it | Download the cleaner it offers
test: some guy online says he'll give me robux if i send a pic | Don't, block him and tell a parent | Send one, it's only a picture | Send it but hide your face | Ask for the robux first, then send him the picture
test: did u hear?? a man in a van told a kid at the park he had puppies in the back, the kid ran off and told his mum. what's the lesson | Never go with someone you don't know; run to a safe adult | Only go if the puppies are real | Go if the man seems really kind and the puppies sound real | It's fine if a friend comes too
test: my nan says if u don't forward that chain message "something terrible happens in 3 days, it happened to her friend". what's true | Nothing bad happens; chain messages only spread worry | Bad luck comes to anyone who breaks the chain, it's happened loads | Forwarding it to ten people keeps you safe | It's true for messages sent at night
test: everyone's saying Sam's dad "got scammed outta his whole house" with gift cards, apparently. pass it on? | No, it's a rumour and would hurt Sam's family | Yes, so people laugh at his dad | Yes, and add how much it was and who told you about it | Yes, but say you heard it from Sam
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a request for payment by means of gift cards? | Genuine businesses never request it, since gift cards cannot be traced or refunded | It is the customary method of paying official fines | It is the safest and most trusted method of paying a stranger online | It is required by law for online purchases
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a chain message threatening misfortune need not be forwarded. | No misfortune results from declining to forward it | Forwarding it incurs a charge to the sender of the original message | The message expires after a single day | Only messages bearing a logo need be forwarded
exam: It is given that a pop-up window declares «Virus detected! Call this number». Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The page shall be closed, an adult informed and the number never called | The number shall be called promptly so that the virus may be removed | The device shall be switched off permanently | The pop-up shall be shared with friends as a warning
exam: State the appropriate conduct where an online contact proposes moving to a private application or meeting in person. | The child shall decline and inform a parent | The child shall agree provided the contact seems friendly | The child shall meet only in a public place alone | The child shall move apps but refuse to meet
